1
00:00:00,371 --> 00:00:10,278
[SPEAKER_02]: Question that keeps coming up various places about relationships.

2
00:00:10,558 --> 00:00:12,399
[SPEAKER_02]: What is... Like romantic relationships.

3
00:00:12,419 --> 00:00:13,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Romantic relationships.

4
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:16,161
[SPEAKER_02]: Intimate relationships.

5
00:00:16,482 --> 00:00:20,384
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we're not... not all relationships are bad.

6
00:00:22,365 --> 00:00:24,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, depends from what point of view you look at it.

7
00:00:24,087 --> 00:00:29,270
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but... compassionate, intimate, sexual.

8
00:00:30,635 --> 00:00:32,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Romantic relationships.

9
00:00:33,236 --> 00:00:34,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that good or bad?

10
00:00:34,336 --> 00:00:34,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

11
00:00:35,017 --> 00:00:36,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, depends on what your goals are.

12
00:00:37,217 --> 00:00:38,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Feels nice.

13
00:00:38,918 --> 00:00:39,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Depends on what your goals are.

14
00:00:40,619 --> 00:00:41,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling nice might be bad.

15
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:44,060
[SPEAKER_01]: But if that's your goal.

16
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:44,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

17
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:47,881
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's your goal, then it's not bad for you.

18
00:00:49,322 --> 00:00:53,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to recognize what you want from your life.

19
00:00:55,304 --> 00:00:57,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you want to find

20
00:00:59,635 --> 00:01:05,523
[SPEAKER_01]: A relationship, invest effort in it, develop it, cultivate it, or do you want to practice dhamma?

21
00:01:07,707 --> 00:01:16,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't want to practice dhamma, or if you only want to practice dhamma partially, then it's not correct to say that the relationships are bad, because you wouldn't see that.

22
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:28,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But you cannot expect then, oh, so my relationships are not bad, but I can still practice the dhamma.

23
00:01:29,524 --> 00:01:31,726
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to recognize they're mutually exclusive.

24
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:39,572
[SPEAKER_02]: But should you not cultivate any?

25
00:01:40,813 --> 00:01:42,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Should you not practice the dhamma?

26
00:01:42,675 --> 00:01:43,556
[SPEAKER_01]: That's then the question.

27
00:01:44,236 --> 00:01:44,917
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's up to you.

28
00:01:48,594 --> 00:01:50,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, up to everyone individually.

29
00:01:50,936 --> 00:01:52,998
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to practice the dhamma, then yes.

30
00:01:53,438 --> 00:01:54,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Relationships are unwholesome.

31
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:57,281
[SPEAKER_01]: They are the opposite of that goal.

32
00:01:58,362 --> 00:02:01,345
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't want to practice the dhamma, then doesn't matter what I say.

33
00:02:01,985 --> 00:02:09,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but if you practice the dhamma and then you don't cultivate relationships, then you'll be lonely and suffer.

34
00:02:10,372 --> 00:02:12,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but at least you try to practice dhamma.

35
00:02:12,354 --> 00:02:12,594
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

36
00:02:13,515 --> 00:02:14,235
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have it all.

37
00:02:14,295 --> 00:02:14,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

38
00:02:15,158 --> 00:02:22,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you either choose the worldly goals, or you choose to overcome the world, in which case means you cannot invest the same effort in the worldly goals.

39
00:02:23,245 --> 00:02:28,070
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, it's, it's, it's, it's sort of contradicting each other.

40
00:02:28,891 --> 00:02:34,496
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the question really... it's not a right question to say, oh, our relationship is bad.

41
00:02:35,957 --> 00:02:40,862
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, should we all practice the dhamma or not, or should I practice the dhamma or not?

42
00:02:41,608 --> 00:02:45,811
[SPEAKER_01]: If the answer is yes, then you got your answers for all the relationships, sexual or otherwise.

43
00:02:46,032 --> 00:02:47,173
[SPEAKER_01]: They are bad.

44
00:02:47,653 --> 00:02:49,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they are against the principle of dhamma.

45
00:02:51,516 --> 00:03:04,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you know you want to practice dhamma, but now wonder why a relationship is bad, against the point of view of the dhamma, well, the answer is you cannot have a relationship, you cannot have intimacy without desire.

46
00:03:06,628 --> 00:03:08,830
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot have desire without lust.

47
00:03:10,228 --> 00:03:16,172
[SPEAKER_01]: And dhamma is freedom from lust, freedom from desire, freedom from intimacy, freedom from depending on anything external.

48
00:03:17,613 --> 00:03:22,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and... Can you have a relationship with others non-externally?

49
00:03:22,337 --> 00:03:22,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

50
00:03:23,437 --> 00:03:26,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have a relationship with others without having desire towards them?

51
00:03:26,359 --> 00:03:26,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

52
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:33,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have relationship, sexual or otherwise, without lust towards their form, externally?

53
00:03:34,325 --> 00:03:35,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

54
00:03:35,926 --> 00:03:36,907
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, even ask yourself,

55
00:03:38,667 --> 00:03:44,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you be in love with somebody without being physically, as well, having lust towards their form?

56
00:03:45,231 --> 00:03:47,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if their form is beautiful or ugly.

57
00:03:48,072 --> 00:03:49,933
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is you want that form.

58
00:03:50,894 --> 00:03:56,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you have desire and lust towards the opposite sex, for example, if their form does not exist?

59
00:03:58,118 --> 00:03:58,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable!

60
00:04:00,017 --> 00:04:09,083
[SPEAKER_01]: You can try to be in love with the idea, but even then that idea assumes a certain form, which is how you find that idea in others, when you fall in love and so on.

61
00:04:09,844 --> 00:04:15,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you already carry that framework, template of this is what I value, and then you recognize it embodied in others.

62
00:04:16,228 --> 00:04:21,411
[SPEAKER_01]: But it has to be external, which means it has to require infatuation, lust and desire.

63
00:04:22,652 --> 00:04:25,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Because dhamma can only be practiced in solitude.

64
00:04:25,634 --> 00:04:26,835
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no group practice.

65
00:04:32,042 --> 00:04:35,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever the Buddha was... numerous suttas, just take a pick.

66
00:04:37,246 --> 00:04:41,329
[SPEAKER_01]: He's told monks to go in solitude and be alone, physically alone.

67
00:04:41,709 --> 00:04:47,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Twelve most of the time in solitude, not in company, even if it's company of other like-minded people.

68
00:04:48,033 --> 00:04:48,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Company is company.

69
00:04:50,615 --> 00:04:53,017
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you have a relationship without company?

70
00:04:54,258 --> 00:04:54,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

71
00:04:58,181 --> 00:05:00,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if you have a relationship, you can't practice the dhamma.

72
00:05:05,238 --> 00:05:09,622
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's saying that where there is intimacy, lust will find the opening.

73
00:05:09,742 --> 00:05:09,942
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

74
00:05:10,082 --> 00:05:12,104
[SPEAKER_02]: And it comes from a really intense sutta.

75
00:05:12,144 --> 00:05:14,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have intimacy without company?

76
00:05:16,948 --> 00:05:18,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there's no company, no room for intimacy.

77
00:05:19,070 --> 00:05:19,190
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

78
00:05:19,410 --> 00:05:22,693
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no intimacy, how can there be lust arising?

79
00:05:28,298 --> 00:05:32,682
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why are relationships bad from the dhamma point of view?

80
00:05:33,550 --> 00:05:38,194
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because they require company.

81
00:05:38,254 --> 00:05:39,515
[SPEAKER_02]: Intimacy?

82
00:05:39,535 --> 00:05:40,416
[SPEAKER_01]: No, company.

83
00:05:40,476 --> 00:05:40,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

84
00:05:41,096 --> 00:05:43,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Which results in intimacy, which results in lust.

85
00:05:43,659 --> 00:05:43,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

86
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:54,428
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you live withdrawn from relationships, if you cultivate a relationship mentally, and wish for it, or refuse to give it up, you're living in company.

87
00:05:57,210 --> 00:05:58,351
[SPEAKER_01]: You are living in intimacy.

88
00:06:03,488 --> 00:06:04,592
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's why they're bad.

89
00:06:07,221 --> 00:06:10,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else that can come out of that relationship, good or bad, is secondary.

90
00:06:11,467 --> 00:06:15,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all of that is founded upon the fact that it requires company of others.

91
00:06:15,911 --> 00:06:22,958
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is obviously, if you want to live a worldly life, then there are ways to cultivate good relationships.

92
00:06:23,438 --> 00:06:24,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, but that's not what we're talking about.

93
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:25,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

94
00:06:25,901 --> 00:06:26,301
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not...

95
00:06:26,361 --> 00:06:28,683
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't... Those are two different things.

96
00:06:29,304 --> 00:06:35,470
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't use two different things to extrapolate the context that would then apply to both, because they're mutually exclusive.

97
00:06:36,411 --> 00:06:46,914
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't want to practice the dhamma, then the statement that all relationships are bad because they require company does not apply to you, because you don't want to practice for arahantship.

98
00:06:48,115 --> 00:06:53,676
[SPEAKER_01]: But that also means that disqualifies you from talking about practice towards arahantship, from talking about dhamma.

99
00:06:54,877 --> 00:06:56,077
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's really the problem.

100
00:06:57,312 --> 00:07:00,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, same like when people like, you know, oh, lay life or a monk.

101
00:07:01,214 --> 00:07:02,034
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about that.

102
00:07:02,675 --> 00:07:06,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if you're a lay person or you're a monk, whether you're in relationships or not.

103
00:07:06,777 --> 00:07:10,319
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is whether you took practice of dhamma as your goal.

104
00:07:11,499 --> 00:07:15,121
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, then it doesn't matter whether you wear robes or not.

105
00:07:15,221 --> 00:07:17,622
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is whether you pursue sensuality or not.

106
00:07:19,123 --> 00:07:24,386
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the goal of dhamma is taken up, pursue of sensuality has to be abandoned.

107
00:07:25,321 --> 00:07:27,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it doesn't matter whether you're a layman or not.

108
00:07:33,324 --> 00:07:34,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where things are rooted.

109
00:07:35,045 --> 00:07:54,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But the the problem, or rather the the worst thing, is the view that I've taken the dhamma, but then, at the same time, assume that you can still engage with sensuality and company and so on, which are two mutually, because you're a layman.

110
00:07:55,568 --> 00:07:55,748
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

111
00:07:56,288 --> 00:07:56,428
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

112
00:07:57,628 --> 00:08:01,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Being a layman does not be... does not mean not being celibate.

113
00:08:05,230 --> 00:08:11,451
[SPEAKER_01]: If being a layman means not being a celibate, then, basically, if you become a celibate, even without wearing a robe, you are not a layman.

114
00:08:12,091 --> 00:08:13,011
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's absolutely fine.

115
00:08:13,071 --> 00:08:14,151
[SPEAKER_01]: You can regard yourself like that.

116
00:08:14,451 --> 00:08:19,892
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Guru used the word ārya-saṅgha even for ordained or non-ordained people, but people with the right view or more.

117
00:08:21,312 --> 00:08:24,553
[SPEAKER_01]: People who have overcome āthavāda, sensuality and so on.

118
00:08:26,290 --> 00:08:29,312
[SPEAKER_01]: And they were not ordained, yet they were the saṅgha of that kind.

119
00:08:29,872 --> 00:08:36,177
[SPEAKER_01]: But he did not use that to include people who were central, people who are not practicing towards dhamma, people who don't have the right view.

120
00:08:36,717 --> 00:08:45,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody shorter than Sotāpanna belongs to... well, dhamma-nuṣārī, sadhana-nuṣārī, they belong to Ārya-saṅga as well, which is the Sotāpanna without still quite realizing the fruit of it.

121
00:08:51,407 --> 00:08:54,269
[SPEAKER_02]: So, a sign that you are

122
00:08:55,797 --> 00:09:09,341
[SPEAKER_02]: Practicing, practicing for intimacy, is something, yeah, I don't know, I've got this thought, that if you are trying to get personal with someone... Well, it's intimate.

123
00:09:09,602 --> 00:09:10,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

124
00:09:10,682 --> 00:09:16,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you get, again, can you get personal with someone, without that someone?

125
00:09:17,584 --> 00:09:18,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

126
00:09:19,845 --> 00:09:22,046
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're already seeking things out externally.

127
00:09:22,066 --> 00:09:23,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't find them,

128
00:09:27,552 --> 00:09:33,936
[SPEAKER_02]: But that is regarded in many Buddhist circles as a kind of V-practice.

129
00:09:34,677 --> 00:09:39,320
[SPEAKER_02]: It's kind of developing this special friendship, deep...

130
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:40,421
[SPEAKER_02]: Personal, intimate.

131
00:09:40,701 --> 00:09:41,622
[SPEAKER_02]: Intimate friendship.

132
00:09:41,902 --> 00:09:43,923
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, because they don't really know where the practice is.

133
00:09:44,003 --> 00:09:47,646
[SPEAKER_02]: And that intimacy opens you up and opens the other person up.

134
00:09:48,406 --> 00:09:48,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Too lust.

135
00:09:49,026 --> 00:09:50,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, too lust, eventually.

136
00:09:51,207 --> 00:09:51,527
[SPEAKER_02]: It gets...

137
00:09:51,787 --> 00:09:53,307
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same in Christianity.

138
00:09:53,327 --> 00:09:58,408
[SPEAKER_01]: In the Christian mysticism, basically, you develop relationship with God, with your imaginary friend.

139
00:09:59,189 --> 00:10:03,350
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop a full-on relationship of love and devotion.

140
00:10:03,830 --> 00:10:10,391
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason that that relationship does not become lustful is because the form of the other does not exist.

141
00:10:11,251 --> 00:10:13,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it remains left hollow, basically.

142
00:10:13,472 --> 00:10:15,632
[SPEAKER_01]: But the attitude is still there, the same.

143
00:10:17,393 --> 00:10:17,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotional.

144
00:10:20,237 --> 00:10:20,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Infatuated.

145
00:10:23,081 --> 00:10:23,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Dependent.

146
00:10:29,328 --> 00:10:31,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to recognize that dhamma is against all of that.

147
00:10:32,632 --> 00:10:33,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice of dhamma.

148
00:10:39,647 --> 00:10:51,832
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do recognize that, then you realize that the other views, the values towards relationship, towards company, towards sensuality... Sensuality is a form of company, as the Buddha said.

149
00:10:52,452 --> 00:10:55,813
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those relationships are contradictory with the goal that you value.

150
00:10:56,814 --> 00:11:05,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can then choose to ignore that contradiction and then feel qualified in talking about practicing towards arahantship and not even being celibate.

151
00:11:07,551 --> 00:11:14,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you cannot ignore that these two things contradict and then make the effort to be celibate and develop that which you yourself value more.

152
00:11:14,915 --> 00:11:15,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from suffering.

153
00:11:19,157 --> 00:11:25,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the worst thing is to develop the view that you will ignore the contradiction of your own existence.

154
00:11:25,320 --> 00:11:28,181
[SPEAKER_01]: I value freedom from suffering, but I'm also not celibate.

155
00:11:28,681 --> 00:11:34,384
[SPEAKER_01]: I value freedom from suffering, full transparency, undoing of my being, abandoning all the asavasya, kilesas.

156
00:11:34,644 --> 00:11:35,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but I also love company.

157
00:11:36,998 --> 00:11:37,378
[SPEAKER_01]: of others.

158
00:11:40,999 --> 00:11:45,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are plain contradictions.

159
00:11:46,922 --> 00:11:49,863
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, should we all abandon the world and practice without ownership?

160
00:11:51,003 --> 00:11:52,143
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not up to you.

161
00:11:53,044 --> 00:11:53,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Do whatever you want to do.

162
00:11:54,404 --> 00:11:56,645
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do, you have to abandon the world.

163
00:11:58,766 --> 00:12:00,006
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't want to do it, that's fine.

164
00:12:00,046 --> 00:12:00,426
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on you.

165
00:12:00,547 --> 00:12:01,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Your actions, your results.

166
00:12:03,820 --> 00:12:15,082
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't try to kind of ignore the contradiction and then sort of refabricate and try to justify that you don't have to abandon the world and you can still practice for the freedom of the world.

167
00:12:15,122 --> 00:12:16,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom of yourself from the world.

168
00:12:16,862 --> 00:12:18,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's a contradiction in terms.

169
00:12:21,623 --> 00:12:23,764
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a simile of the saw.

170
00:12:24,884 --> 00:12:32,045
[SPEAKER_02]: In one of the sections, the first section, there's a monk called Pāguna.

171
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:36,261
[SPEAKER_02]: And he was entangled with the nuns.

172
00:12:36,581 --> 00:12:38,441
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he was quite intimate with the nuns.

173
00:12:39,762 --> 00:12:44,283
[SPEAKER_02]: If any monk spoke in disgrace of those nuns in his presence...

174
00:12:44,323 --> 00:12:45,424
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he was still a proper monk.

175
00:12:45,444 --> 00:12:47,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which means he was still keeping the rules.

176
00:12:47,724 --> 00:12:49,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he wasn't physical with those nuns or anything else.

177
00:12:49,925 --> 00:12:52,186
[SPEAKER_02]: He was just intimate with them.

178
00:12:52,206 --> 00:12:52,766
[SPEAKER_02]: He was intimate.

179
00:12:52,926 --> 00:12:54,706
[SPEAKER_02]: He was friendly.

180
00:12:54,946 --> 00:12:56,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Having friendship and so on.

181
00:12:57,441 --> 00:13:02,805
[SPEAKER_02]: So, nothing sexual may, you know, overtly... No, because that would have been mentioned.

182
00:13:02,825 --> 00:13:03,065
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

183
00:13:03,965 --> 00:13:06,667
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he was just entangled, intimate with them, so to speak.

184
00:13:08,148 --> 00:13:16,814
[SPEAKER_02]: If any other monk spoke in dispraise of the nuns in his presence, he would be angered and displeased and would make an issue out of it.

185
00:13:17,814 --> 00:13:25,660
[SPEAKER_02]: And if any monk spoke in dispraise of him in the presence of the nuns, they would be angered, displeased and would make an issue out of it.

186
00:13:26,679 --> 00:13:30,163
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how much he was spending time entangled with the nuns.

187
00:13:30,243 --> 00:13:30,443
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

188
00:13:31,384 --> 00:13:34,988
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Could that entanglement arise if he didn't spend time with them?

189
00:13:35,048 --> 00:13:35,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

190
00:13:35,248 --> 00:13:41,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Could that displeasure... Could then everything else that arose from the entanglement arise if he did not spend time in their company?

191
00:13:42,656 --> 00:13:43,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

192
00:13:44,658 --> 00:13:46,500
[SPEAKER_01]: And by the way, they were all very like-minded.

193
00:13:47,492 --> 00:13:50,034
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, they were so like-minded, I bet they clicked right away.

194
00:13:50,634 --> 00:13:53,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Same values, same goals, monk, nun, same, same, same, same, same.

195
00:13:54,277 --> 00:13:56,238
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm celibate, you're celibate, we're perfect for each other.

196
00:13:56,258 --> 00:13:57,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

197
00:13:58,440 --> 00:14:00,361
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the company that is the problem.

198
00:14:03,664 --> 00:14:09,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Because often people try to justify the company on account of a greater goal and greater purpose that we all partake in.

199
00:14:09,928 --> 00:14:12,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, let's all come and sit together in this meditation.

200
00:14:14,795 --> 00:14:19,078
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's through that attachment, aversion is born.

201
00:14:20,319 --> 00:14:20,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

202
00:14:21,580 --> 00:14:30,745
[SPEAKER_01]: If someone tries to hurt those nuns, you get... You will not be able to be indifferent.

203
00:14:33,087 --> 00:14:40,452
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then the Buddha called, went to speak to him, says, come and

204
00:14:47,389 --> 00:14:48,430
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the Buddha questions him.

205
00:14:48,510 --> 00:14:53,433
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it true, Paguna, that you are spending too much time with the nuns?

206
00:14:54,493 --> 00:14:55,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what is time anyway?

207
00:14:56,154 --> 00:14:58,035
[SPEAKER_02]: Spending too much time with you, basically.

208
00:14:58,515 --> 00:14:59,256
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, yes, Lord.

209
00:14:59,576 --> 00:15:06,300
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, aren't you a clansman who has gone forth from the home life into homelessness out of conviction?

210
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:07,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

211
00:15:08,181 --> 00:15:09,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Aren't you the one?

212
00:15:09,082 --> 00:15:09,662
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

213
00:15:10,255 --> 00:15:11,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, he says it's not fitting for you.

214
00:15:11,476 --> 00:15:12,256
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not fitting.

215
00:15:12,836 --> 00:15:15,097
[SPEAKER_01]: It does not fit the values you took upon yourself.

216
00:15:36,775 --> 00:15:41,237
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were not a clansman who has abandoned his lay life and became a monk, I don't care.

217
00:15:41,337 --> 00:15:41,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Do what you like.

218
00:15:44,139 --> 00:15:44,919
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not right.

219
00:15:45,419 --> 00:15:46,320
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why he's telling you.

220
00:15:46,340 --> 00:15:46,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

221
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:47,601
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's not right.

222
00:15:48,261 --> 00:15:59,347
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, so if anyone speaks in disgrace of the nuns in your presence, even then you should abandon any desire related to the household life and any thoughts related to the household life.

223
00:16:00,632 --> 00:16:02,712
[SPEAKER_02]: And, even then, you should train yourself.

224
00:16:03,553 --> 00:16:06,933
[SPEAKER_02]: My mind will be unaffected and I will say no evil words.

225
00:16:06,973 --> 00:16:10,834
[SPEAKER_02]: I will remain sympathetic to that person's welfare.

226
00:16:12,014 --> 00:16:16,775
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not lose the framework of non-ill-will, dispassion, and so on.

227
00:16:17,515 --> 00:16:28,037
[SPEAKER_02]: If anyone gives... hurts those nuns with a stone, or a hand, or a rod, or a knife, in your presence, right there, you should abandon any desire and say the same thing.

228
00:16:29,331 --> 00:16:31,613
[SPEAKER_02]: You should abandon all ill will towards those people.

229
00:16:31,653 --> 00:16:32,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

230
00:16:32,895 --> 00:16:39,221
[SPEAKER_02]: If anyone should speak in dispraise of you, in your presence, even then you should abandon desires.

231
00:16:41,503 --> 00:16:48,350
[SPEAKER_02]: If anyone gives you a hit with a hand, or a stone, or a rod, or a knife, you should abandon all desires.

232
00:16:48,470 --> 00:16:48,850
[SPEAKER_01]: You should know.

233
00:16:48,871 --> 00:16:51,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone what to be said for other people.

234
00:16:52,194 --> 00:16:53,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivate that for others.

235
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:11,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the criteria, then, is not whether you're a layman or a monk.

236
00:17:12,122 --> 00:17:14,042
[SPEAKER_01]: The criteria is whether you want to practice dhamma or not.

237
00:17:18,704 --> 00:17:19,545
[SPEAKER_02]: Should I be doing this?

238
00:17:20,545 --> 00:17:20,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

239
00:17:20,725 --> 00:17:22,466
[SPEAKER_01]: That's also, should lay people be celibate?

240
00:17:23,066 --> 00:17:24,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, if they want to practice dhamma for arahantship.

241
00:17:26,868 --> 00:17:28,909
[SPEAKER_01]: If they say, well, I don't want to practice dhamma for arahantship, fine.

242
00:17:29,129 --> 00:17:32,250
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you're not going to get to talk about dhamma of arahantship.

243
00:17:32,850 --> 00:17:36,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you refuse to practice on that level by refusing to be celibate.

244
00:17:37,715 --> 00:17:39,116
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

245
00:17:39,216 --> 00:17:46,238
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I want to have a view of practicing towards arahantship, and at the same time, do the opposite of the requirement for practice for arahantship.

246
00:17:47,019 --> 00:17:49,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's why you're a living contradiction.

247
00:17:50,827 --> 00:17:52,968
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't want to be... and you find for yourself.

248
00:17:53,028 --> 00:17:53,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait.

249
00:17:54,149 --> 00:17:56,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I a contradiction to my own values?

250
00:17:56,270 --> 00:17:57,071
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what they would ask.

251
00:17:57,371 --> 00:18:02,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Aren't you the man who left his whole life on account of these values?

252
00:18:03,134 --> 00:18:04,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why are you contradicting them?

253
00:18:04,595 --> 00:18:06,737
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't fit your own values.

254
00:18:07,177 --> 00:18:07,597
[SPEAKER_01]: It's okay.

255
00:18:08,658 --> 00:18:10,319
[SPEAKER_01]: They do not fit your own values.

256
00:18:16,648 --> 00:18:23,595
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, oh, the only way you're contradicting yourself is because you ignore or you turn a blind eye to the extent of your own values.

257
00:18:25,216 --> 00:18:26,237
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the worst thing.

258
00:18:27,679 --> 00:18:30,541
[SPEAKER_01]: By turning a blind eye, you develop a view that you can have both.

259
00:18:31,602 --> 00:18:36,307
[SPEAKER_01]: I am qualified to talk about Arahantship and practice the Arahantship, and I also don't have to be a celibate.

260
00:18:37,588 --> 00:18:40,931
[SPEAKER_01]: And deep down, if you look at it closely, you already know it's a contradiction in terms.

261
00:18:43,605 --> 00:18:49,527
[SPEAKER_02]: But I just want to have everything go my own way.

262
00:18:49,567 --> 00:18:51,968
[SPEAKER_01]: If I believe... Well, it's not even that rational.

263
00:18:52,008 --> 00:18:55,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, no, I just don't want to expose myself to any form of discomfort.

264
00:18:56,670 --> 00:19:00,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I just always go with the path of least resistance on the emotional kind.

265
00:19:02,152 --> 00:19:08,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's going to cause some friction internally, if you recognize that your values contradict things you want to do.

266
00:19:09,997 --> 00:19:13,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, I need to practice sense restraint now because it contradicts the values.

267
00:19:14,959 --> 00:19:15,859
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is unpleasant.

268
00:19:16,940 --> 00:19:27,123
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I could either endure that discomfort and grow out of it, or I can ignore or try to remold my values to allow me to engage in things I want to do guilt-free.

269
00:19:28,084 --> 00:19:29,144
[SPEAKER_01]: It always comes down to that.

270
00:19:29,584 --> 00:19:29,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

271
00:19:31,825 --> 00:19:36,407
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if layperson means sensuality, relationship, company, dhamma does not apply to you.

272
00:19:37,926 --> 00:19:44,471
[SPEAKER_01]: But if a layperson gives up dependence on company, intimacy, lust, relationship, sensuality, dhamma applies to you.

273
00:19:44,491 --> 00:19:45,872
[SPEAKER_01]: You partake in the noble saṅgha.

274
00:19:46,913 --> 00:19:47,934
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't have it both ways.

275
00:19:50,275 --> 00:19:54,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Despite what contemporary sort of teachings that you selectively pick up might tell you.

276
00:19:55,319 --> 00:19:56,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Read the suttas and you'll see for yourself.

277
00:19:58,061 --> 00:20:01,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have people these days say, ah, you can be arahant.

278
00:20:03,486 --> 00:20:07,168
[SPEAKER_02]: You can have unpleasant desire and be free from suffering.

279
00:20:07,828 --> 00:20:08,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, exactly.

280
00:20:09,649 --> 00:20:14,472
[SPEAKER_01]: You can feel the burning desire and be free from desire at the same time.

281
00:20:16,193 --> 00:20:19,015
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much what I'm saying.

282
00:20:19,175 --> 00:20:23,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Being free from desire, you can engage with all desires.

283
00:20:24,718 --> 00:20:25,338
[SPEAKER_01]: How does that work?

284
00:20:28,580 --> 00:20:29,420
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that sounds good.

285
00:20:30,825 --> 00:20:32,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Only to the one who is not free from desire.

286
00:20:33,706 --> 00:20:37,508
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have an inkling of freedom from desire, you realize, actually, even that does not sound good.

287
00:20:46,032 --> 00:20:47,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and there was that other suit, similar.

288
00:20:47,873 --> 00:20:51,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, that wasn't necessarily in the context of practicing dhamma.

289
00:20:51,194 --> 00:20:54,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just in the context why intimacy is unwholesome.

290
00:20:55,936 --> 00:20:57,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it makes you dependable.

291
00:20:58,284 --> 00:21:01,124
[SPEAKER_01]: on even a greater extent of things that are not in your control.

292
00:21:03,308 --> 00:21:03,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

293
00:21:03,868 --> 00:21:05,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Love for children, your spouse.

294
00:21:06,270 --> 00:21:08,952
[SPEAKER_01]: If something happens to them, you cannot not be affected.

295
00:21:09,753 --> 00:21:10,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize that.

296
00:21:10,814 --> 00:21:12,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is a liability.

297
00:21:12,375 --> 00:21:21,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I'm already liable to all this suffering, simply on account of my own existence, let alone incorporating existence of others into my own dependence, into my own existence.

298
00:21:21,583 --> 00:21:22,664
[SPEAKER_01]: That makes you really grieved.

299
00:21:22,684 --> 00:21:26,046
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the children are the greatest source of suffering, as the Buddha said.

300
00:21:26,627 --> 00:21:30,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if something happens to your child, there's no way you'll be economous.

301
00:21:31,191 --> 00:21:31,771
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no way.

302
00:21:32,732 --> 00:21:34,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And the suit I'm thinking of was that when...

303
00:21:35,434 --> 00:21:38,576
[SPEAKER_01]: He gives the example why intimacy and love is cause of suffering.

304
00:21:39,276 --> 00:21:45,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Was the man in love with a woman, infatuated, having lust towards her?

305
00:21:46,120 --> 00:21:53,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And he asked the man, wouldn't that... like, if that man were to see that woman laughing and giggling with another man, would he suffer on account of that sight?

306
00:21:54,374 --> 00:21:55,334
[SPEAKER_01]: He's like, yes, of course.

307
00:21:55,434 --> 00:21:55,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

308
00:21:55,975 --> 00:22:00,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Because his mind is bound with her form by lust infatuation.

309
00:22:00,776 --> 00:22:06,558
[SPEAKER_01]: And now that form is basically given to somebody else, at least in his mind, as in he has no control over it.

310
00:22:06,898 --> 00:22:07,098
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

311
00:22:08,459 --> 00:22:16,042
[SPEAKER_01]: It's extreme displeasure, not because of what she's doing, but because of he's bound with her that requires certain behavior that she's now going against.

312
00:22:17,022 --> 00:22:19,883
[SPEAKER_01]: And says, if that man has wisdom, he will reflect thus.

313
00:22:20,922 --> 00:22:26,425
[SPEAKER_01]: He would say, hmm, my mind is bound by infatuation and lust, love towards that woman.

314
00:22:27,425 --> 00:22:31,187
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when I see her with another man, I experience extreme pain and suffering.

315
00:22:31,727 --> 00:22:36,310
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I were to undo that lust and infatuation towards that woman?

316
00:22:37,190 --> 00:22:47,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he sets himself upon not cultivating that lust, not welcoming that infatuation, not clinging to it, basically allowing it to endure, but basically overcoming it, not fueling it anymore.

317
00:22:47,914 --> 00:22:57,781
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, later on, on another occasion, he doesn't have lust and infatuation with that woman, and if he were to see that woman laughing and giggling, would he experience pain and suffering?

318
00:22:57,881 --> 00:22:58,041
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

319
00:22:58,662 --> 00:23:00,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's nothing binding him to that form.

320
00:23:01,283 --> 00:23:03,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing requiring a certain set of behavior of that form.

321
00:23:04,586 --> 00:23:06,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, suffering cannot arise.

322
00:23:07,608 --> 00:23:07,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

323
00:23:07,868 --> 00:23:10,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you suffer on account of losing a child, if you don't have a child?

324
00:23:13,223 --> 00:23:14,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

325
00:23:14,404 --> 00:23:17,625
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have a child, could you theoretically overcome any attachment towards a child?

326
00:23:17,685 --> 00:23:17,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

327
00:23:18,866 --> 00:23:25,370
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to overcome the slightest attachment, slightest infatuation, slightest love towards your own child, could you suffer on account of anything happening to that child?

328
00:23:25,770 --> 00:23:26,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

329
00:23:27,291 --> 00:23:28,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a choice.

330
00:23:29,352 --> 00:23:33,114
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll ignore that possibility of development and cultivate intimacy.

331
00:23:34,495 --> 00:23:34,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintain it.

332
00:23:35,755 --> 00:23:38,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignore it, which is a form of maintaining it already.

333
00:23:39,694 --> 00:23:43,995
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you make yourself liable to anything wholesome or unwholesome that can affect you.

334
00:23:44,315 --> 00:23:48,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so why don't... why don't I suffer when other children die or...

335
00:23:49,237 --> 00:23:49,397
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

336
00:23:49,417 --> 00:23:50,397
[SPEAKER_02]: ...other people's mothers die?

337
00:23:50,417 --> 00:24:01,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there was that... there was that sutta where the Buddha was sitting in the forest with the monks and said, monks, would you be upset if people come in now, in this woodland where we're sitting, and start collecting these twigs and branches and burning them and breaking them?

338
00:24:01,821 --> 00:24:01,981
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

339
00:24:02,401 --> 00:24:03,301
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we wouldn't be upset.

340
00:24:03,361 --> 00:24:04,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would we be upset?

341
00:24:04,762 --> 00:24:06,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Who cares about these discarded branches?

342
00:24:07,263 --> 00:24:08,563
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you should regard your own body.

343
00:24:09,903 --> 00:24:10,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Your own life.

344
00:24:11,524 --> 00:24:15,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone body of others which are already secondary to your point of view.

345
00:24:18,266 --> 00:24:20,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, like discarded twigs and branches.

346
00:24:20,146 --> 00:24:25,208
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you need to regard your whole experience as a whole.

347
00:24:26,188 --> 00:24:28,189
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how we'll experience this passion towards that.

348
00:24:28,719 --> 00:24:32,202
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's an attachment to other things.

349
00:24:32,262 --> 00:24:35,584
[SPEAKER_02]: You're exposing yourself so much more to suffering.

350
00:24:35,604 --> 00:24:37,206
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you're just expanding your exposure.

351
00:24:37,226 --> 00:24:38,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, also, exactly.

352
00:24:38,106 --> 00:24:46,113
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot be attached in love, infatuated with others, without ignoring your own existence to that extent.

353
00:24:47,349 --> 00:24:49,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Without ignoring your own liability.

354
00:24:49,910 --> 00:24:54,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Like your own existence makes you liable to suffering on account of things you're not in control of.

355
00:24:54,750 --> 00:25:02,352
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to ignore that in order to allow yourself to proliferate far enough to actually incorporate others in your love and infatuation.

356
00:25:04,672 --> 00:25:12,374
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible to become infatuated and in love if you don't forget the context of your own existence, which is a perilous situation.

357
00:25:15,460 --> 00:25:24,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to be repetitively ignoring your own nature of your own situation, which is impermanent and suffering, in order to basically establish your mind upon other people.

358
00:25:31,233 --> 00:25:37,839
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember someone saying that he was practicing metta for many, many years.

359
00:25:38,359 --> 00:25:40,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Metta in the traditional sense.

360
00:25:41,962 --> 00:25:44,044
[SPEAKER_02]: And... but when he had a child,

361
00:25:45,554 --> 00:25:50,638
[SPEAKER_02]: That just went right out the window, because it was when the child was bullied at school.

362
00:25:50,879 --> 00:25:51,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

363
00:25:51,199 --> 00:25:52,120
[SPEAKER_02]: But you want to resist.

364
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:53,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

365
00:25:53,020 --> 00:25:54,482
[SPEAKER_02]: Destroy the other children, you know.

366
00:25:56,924 --> 00:26:06,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because if you were to practice metta, you wouldn't have been engaging in acts of love, lust, sensuality and everything else.

367
00:26:07,112 --> 00:26:09,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all of that means acting out of how you feel.

368
00:26:10,255 --> 00:26:13,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you act out of how you feel, means you're not equanimous towards what you feel.

369
00:26:15,707 --> 00:26:17,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no metta, there's no framework.

370
00:26:17,889 --> 00:26:18,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

371
00:26:19,991 --> 00:26:22,754
[SPEAKER_02]: He was practicing the wrong type of metta, actually.

372
00:26:23,355 --> 00:26:25,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, which really isn't metta.

373
00:26:25,357 --> 00:26:25,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

